+-----------------------------------------------------------------------
| Running ${PKGSTEM} on OpenBSD
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------

This experimental port runs 64-bit Windows applications on OpenBSD/amd64.
It does not provide 32-bit Windows support.

Initialize Wine
===============

Without WINEPREFIX, Wine stores its configuration in $HOME/.wine.
To initialize this prefix:

    wineboot -i
    wineserver -w

PulseAudio
==========

Wine uses PulseAudio, which sends audio to sndio on OpenBSD. Run it as the
same user as Wine. To restart the user's PulseAudio daemon:

    pulseaudio --kill
    pulseaudio --start

Inspect the server and its playback devices:

    pactl info
    pactl list short sinks

The usual hardware output is sndio-sink. auto_null is a dummy output:
audio sent there will not reach the speakers. If sndio-sink is listed:

    pactl set-default-sink sndio-sink
    pactl set-sink-mute sndio-sink 0
    pactl set-sink-volume sndio-sink 100%

Check the volume controls between PulseAudio and the audio device:

    sndioctl

The per-application control name can vary. Seeing PulseAudio in sndioctl
confirms its connection to sndiod, but does not prove that Wine is sending
audible samples.

Steam
=====

The package installs wine-steam in ${LOCALBASE}/bin, with unzip and the
Liberation fonts as dependencies. This helper prepares Steam for this
64-bit-only port: it installs the client directly from Valve's update
packages, without running the Windows installer, and applies the prefix,
font and resource-limit settings described below.

As your normal desktop user, start PulseAudio as described above, then run:

    wine-steam

On the first run, it creates $HOME/.wine-steam and initializes Wine without
installing Mono or Gecko. It downloads the Windows 64-bit client packages
listed in Valve's manifest, verifies their SHA-256 checksums and extracts
them into:

    $HOME/.wine-steam/drive_c/Steam

An Internet connection is required for installation. No separate wineboot
command is needed. The helper always uses this dedicated prefix, separate
from the default $HOME/.wine prefix.

At each launch, it registers Liberation replacements for Arial, Times New
Roman and Courier New, raises the data-segment and open-file limits to the
user's existing hard limits, then starts Steam with -cef-disable-sandbox
(disabling the CEF browser sandbox).

Run the same command to start Steam again. If installation was interrupted,
run it again to retry. Additional command-line arguments are passed to Steam.

Debugging
=========

WINEDEBUG selects Wine's diagnostic messages at process startup. Set it for
one command, then reproduce the problem. Restart the application to change
the selection; existing processes keep their settings.

Each comma-separated option has the following form, without spaces:

    [program.exe:][class]+channel
    [program.exe:][class]-channel

The optional class selects one kind of message:

    err     A detected error; the application may still recover.
    warn    A suspicious condition or a possible problem.
    fixme   Missing or incomplete Wine functionality; not necessarily fatal.
    trace   Detailed execution messages, usually much more verbose.

These are independent classes, not verbosity levels. With WINEDEBUG unset,
Wine enables err and fixme on all channels. Options modify these defaults:

For an application that fails to start or crashes, begin with:

    WINEDEBUG=+timestamp,+pid,+seh,+loaddll,+process \
        wine-steam > "$HOME/wine.log" 2>&1

The default err/fixme messages remain enabled.
Replace wine-steam with wine /path/to/application.exe for another program.

These options add context to message prefixes:

- +timestamp: adds a time counter in seconds.milliseconds to measure delays.
- +pid: adds the Windows process ID to distinguish Steam and its children.

For example, 123.456:0020:0024: identifies the timestamp,
process 0020 and thread 0024 (the thread ID is already printed by default).
These IDs are hexadecimal Windows IDs, not the Unix process IDs shown by ps.

These channels add diagnostic messages about specific operations:

- +seh: traces exceptions and their handling.
- +loaddll: shows which DLLs are loaded.
- +process: traces process creation.

An exception in a seh trace may be handled by the application; its presence
alone does not prove a crash.

For other symptoms, replace the three diagnostic channels above with a
relevant group. Capture only the steps needed to reproduce the problem:

- Missing sound: +mmdevapi,+pulse shows audio devices, stream creation and
  communication with PulseAudio. Add +xaudio2 for a game using XAudio2.
- Black screen or graphics errors: +dxgi,+d3d11 for display resources and
  Direct3D 11 calls. Add +d3d for WineD3D, or +opengl,+wgl for OpenGL and
  context creation. These traces can become very large while rendering.
- Window management: +x11drv for the interaction between Wine and X11.
- Video playback: +mfplat,+quartz for Media Foundation and DirectShow paths.
- Missing fonts or slow font initialization: +font,+dwrite for GDI and
  DirectWrite. Font enumeration can produce a large amount of output.
- File access or registry problems: +file,+reg to see paths, keys and results.
- Allocation or mapping failures: +virtual to inspect virtual address ranges
  and protection changes. This is separate from heap validation below.
- Network problems: +winsock,+dnsapi for sockets and DNS. Add +winhttp or
  +wininet only when the application uses the corresponding HTTP API.
- Suspected synchronization problems: +thread,+sync,+server for threads,
  waits and Wine server requests. Use a short capture; idle waits are normal
  and this output alone may not identify a deadlock.

Avoid broad instrumentation
---------------------------

Do not use +all for routine diagnosis. It changes execution as well as
logging: +relay instruments calls and returns through Wine DLL exports,
while trace+heap enables full heap validation during heap operations.
warn+heap also enables extra checks, so warn+all is not a neutral way to
collect more messages. Heap checks add memory markers and delay some frees;
they can greatly slow programs and expose different failures.

+all also enables d3d_sync, which forces WineD3D command serialization and
synchronous OpenGL debug output. This changes graphics timing; enable it
only when investigating synchronization in WineD3D.

Use heap checks only when specifically investigating memory corruption,
preferably with a small reproducer. trace-heap,warn-heap disables the checks
enabled by those debug classes while preserving err/fixme heap messages.
This only disables heap checks enabled by WINEDEBUG; checks requested through
Windows registry settings or a debugger may remain active.

Reserve +relay for short, targeted API investigations. It can produce
gigabytes of output during font enumeration alone. +snoop is not useful on
this amd64-only port: its call instrumentation is implemented for i386.

Large logs can fill the disk. Deleted logs still occupy space while any
process holds them open, even while sleeping. Use fstat(1) to find these
processes, ps(1) to inspect them, and kill(1) to terminate them.

Official Wine documentation
--------------------------

- Debug channels and WINEDEBUG:
  https://gitlab.winehq.org/wine/wine/-/wikis/Debug-Channels
- Developer's Guide (see "Debugging Wine"):
  https://gitlab.winehq.org/wine/wine/-/wikis/Wine-Developer's-Guide
- Documentation index:
  https://gitlab.winehq.org/wine/wine/-/wikis/Documentation

The installed wine(1) manual also documents WINEDEBUG under ENVIRONMENT
VARIABLES. Its broad logging examples should be used with the heap and
relay precautions above in mind.
